Repeating commands
Often, you want to apply the same operation to several variables. You can write out the commands by hand, but this is repetitive and easy to mistype.
1a. Repeat a variable construction for multiple variables

data &lt;- data |&gt;
  mutate(
    rgood1 = 1 - good1,
    rgood2 = 1 - good2,
    rgood3 = 1 - good3,
    rgood4 = 1 - good4,
    rgood5 = 1 - good5
  )

This works, but it is not very efficient. If you later discover that the transformation should be different, you have to change five lines of code.
1b. Equivalent, using across()

data &lt;- data |&gt;
  mutate(
    across(
      good1:good5,
      ~ 1 - .x,
      .names = "r{.col}"
    )
  )

The function across() applies the same operation to several variables. Here, good1:good5 selects the variables from good1 to good5. The expression ~ 1 - .x means: take each selected variable and compute 1 - variable. The argument .names = "r{.col}" creates new variable names by adding r before the original variable name.
1c. Equivalent, selecting variables by name pattern

data &lt;- data |&gt;
  mutate(
    across(
      starts_with("good"),
      ~ 1 - .x,
      .names = "r{.col}"
    )
  )

This does the same thing, but selects variables by name. All variables whose names start with good are transformed. This is useful when there are many variables with a shared naming pattern.
1d. Equivalent, looping over a numerical range

for (i in 1:5) {
  data[[paste0("rgood", i)]] &lt;- 1 - data[[paste0("good", i)]]
}

This loop repeats the same command for i = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. The function paste0() creates variable names such as good1 and rgood1. This approach is useful, but for data manipulation in the tidyverse, across() is often easier to read.




for (i in 1:5) {

  data |&gt;
    filter(country == i) |&gt;
    summarise(
      mean_income = mean(income, na.rm = TRUE)
    )

  data |&gt;
    filter(country == i) |&gt;
    count(religion)

}

This loop repeats two analyses for each value of country from 1 to 5. For each country, it computes the mean income and creates a frequency table of religion. This is useful when you want to run the same checks separately for multiple groups.

Assignments
In these assignments, you should use the dataset sbs399. Solutions can be found in dh_select.Rmd.

Using filter() and nrow(), count the number of observations in sbs399:

with age at most 30;
with age at least 20;
with age between 20 and 30, boundaries included;
with age between 20 and 30, boundaries excluded;
males over 25;
people who are either below 68 or female, with sex equal to 1.

Select the subjects with age at most 30 and store this subset in a new object. Do not overwrite the original dataset sbs399.
Count the number of subjects of age at least 20 in this subset.
Separately for men and women, count the number of subjects of age at least 20. Use .by or group_by() for this last question.
Reload the full dataset sbs399.
Create a selection variable, say touse, that takes the value 1 for males over 30 and 0 otherwise.
For the selected sample:

create a two-way table;
compute the correlation between the respondent’s education, educ, and the respondent’s father’s education, feduc.

What is the difference between the following R commands?

data |&gt;   
  select(-sex)  

data |&gt;   
  filter(sex != 1)  

data |&gt;   
  filter(sex == 1) 

Using the household identifier hrespnr in the dataset sbs399, use .by or group_by() to:

define the number of participants in a household;
list the oldest people in households without creating a new variable;
list the ages of the same-sex households;
list sex and age in increasing order of age in households where the age difference is larger than 5, without creating new variables;
drop the singles.
